## Introduction
Multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme cascade reactions include at least two consecutive processes in one pot, exhibiting notable advantages that do not require separation or purification of intermediates, thereby reducing the byproducts generation
and processing costs.[1] These biologically derived processes have attracted significant commercial interest because of their environmentally benign generation of valuable chemicals and materials.[1,2] However, their industrial scale application is still hampered by the high production costs and restricted operating conditions of natural enzymes, such as poor stability in acidic, thermal conditions and long-term storage, intolerance to toxic products, as well as low efficient in recovery and reuse.[3] To realize the efficient concerted assembly of multienzyme or cofactor-enzyme in one pot, efficient immobilization strategy on solid matrices is an effective way to enhance enzymes and cofactors compartmentalization, stability, recovery as well as reusability.[4]
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted considerable attention for enzymes and cofactors immobilization,[5-12] because of their distinctive properties including structural diversity, ultrahigh surface area and porosity, as well as uniform and controllable surface chemistry and pore size,[13-16] which enables size-selective diffusion of reactants to the active site through their tailorable pore network, meanwhile protects enzymes and cofactors from many inhospitable environments.[9-11] However, the current immobilization strategies of enzymes within MOFs are still facing significant limitations. For example, the enzyme structure/function may be perturbated by covalently linking enzymes to a surface;[17] enzyme leaching and random orientation may occur by physical adsorption; and undesired enzyme conformational change could be resulted by steric trapping or coprecipitation/biomineralization
within MOFs.[18,19] Moreover, the non-specific or specific interaction of the MOF and enzymes could alter the native state of the enzymes and may further disturb their activity.[5-7,19,20] In addition, the intrinsic open channels of most MOFs are generally smaller than \(2\ \mathsf{nm}\),[21] which significantly limit the diffusion efficiency of large substrates, cofactors and products. Therefore, achieving highly efficient multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme cascades are challenging in conventional biocatalytic MOF systems without optimizing the conformation and spatial distribution of the encapsulated enzymes, accessibility of reactants, as well as the diffusion of substrates, cofactors and products,[22] which are key principles of element design in an optimal biocatalytic MOF microreactor.
Creating meso-/macro voids inside the nanoporous MOFs enables the encapsulation of large-sized guest species in its relatively free state in the large pores while improving the substrate diffusion.[23] So far, various bottom-up and top-down approaches have been reported to generate large voids or defects in pristine nanoporous MOFs as hierarchically porous supports for enzyme immobilization.[24-31] Among them, perturbation-assisted[32,33] and template-directed synthesis[34,35] are the primary bottom-up strategies for generating hierarchical pores within MOFs. Other methods include linker labilization,[36] introducing modulator/surfactant,[37] or tuning the ligand-to-metal ratio.[31] Although these methods offer an Ab initio strategy to synthesize hierarchically porous MOFs, there is a "trade-off" relationship between the meso-/macro porosity and the framework integrity, which could lead to partial amorphization or collapse of the MOF framework. In addition, their versatility and scalability, as important prerequisites for future industrial exploitation, has not yet been reported.
Etching is an appropriate top-down approach for generating voids within solid matrices. Water has been used as an etching agent to induce mesopores within MOFs.[38] However, the etching occurred via the MOF surfaces and the voids are essentially out-of-control. Recently, we constructed a series of hollow MOFs via surface-protected etching by weak acidic phenolic compounds.[21] Among them, tannic acid (TA) demonstrated a synergistic effect of surface protection and etching of the MOF, where the macro-sized cavities inside MOFs were emerged by the slowly released protons from TA, while the TA coated and protected the exterior MOF surface from structurally collapsing.[21,39] Compared to the previous methods,[32-37] this method is facile, low-cost, and less-time consuming. Importantly, it is compatible with most substrates because of the high binding affinity of TA to diverse surfaces, which has shown potential as a host platform for enzyme immobilization.[40] However, the complex interfacial interactions of enzyme and hierarchically porous MOFs are rarely revealed. Very recently, Tsung and co-workers probed the interactions between encapsulated enzyme and hollow-shell MOF matrices.[25] Their results suggested that the encapsulated enzyme presented a less constrained conformation within hollow MOFs, as determined from infrared (IR) spectroscopy.[23] Nevertheless, enzyme aggregation could still occur inevitably in the overly expanded space.[40,41] Although IR technique could provide some useful information for changes in enzyme conformation in the confined space,[5,29,23] the understanding of the interfacial interactions between organic ligands, metal ions, enzymes and the etching reagent during and after the etching process from atomic level is required, which is important for the
design of ideal hierarchically porous MOFs as functional matrices for the multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalysis.
Herein, a hierarchically porous zeolitic imidazolate framework system (ZIF-L, composed of unique two-dimensional layered structures crystallizing in the orthorhombic space group Cmce) containing bi-enzyme was first constructed, where the mesoporous size range was precisely tuned by controlling the etching time by TA. Using a combination of complementary characterization techniques, the interfacial interactions between the enzymes, the MOF framework, and the etching reagent, were revealed. Then, these optimized hierarchically porous MOF microreactors were employed as versatile platforms for improving desired multienzyme and cofactor-dependent biocatalysis. Expanding the pore size of the biocatalytic MOF can provide sufficient space for multienzyme or cofactor-enzyme reorientation and spreading within MOFs in their lower surface energy state, and accelerate the diffusion of substrates and intermediates, resulting up to 16-fold higher enzymatic activity than the pristine biocatalytic MOFs. In addition, the resistance capacity to various commercially relevant conditions (i.e. low pH, high temperature, and hydrolase) were significantly improved from 29-62% to 68.7-90%. As we progress towards the better understanding of the interfacial interactions of MOF and enzyme for accelerating the biocatalytic activity of multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme and their stabilization within porous materials, a more "off-the-shelf" enzyme immobilization strategy hopefully could be offered soon for commercially viable chemical transformations.
## Results and Discussion
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of the obtained biocomposites indicated that the glucose oxidase (GOx)-horseradish peroxidase (HRP) bi-enzyme-ZIF-L after TA treatment retained the same morphology as those without TA etching, along with a similar particle size of 1.0-1.3 μm (Figure 1a and Figure S1). However, significant internal voids appeared after the etching process, as revealed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Figure 1b). The energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping confirmed the Zn, N, and O elements were distributed through-out the TA-etched particles (Figure S2). Synchrotron small angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns demonstrated that no extra diffraction peak from impurities was found, confirming that the pristine ZIF-L crystal structure was preserved after TA etching (Figure 1c and Figure S3). The TA coating on ZIF-L was confirmed by the Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra. After TA treatment, two extra adsorption bands emerged at ~1340 cm⁻¹ and 1700 cm⁻¹ for both the bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA and the control ZIF-L-TA (no enzymes), corresponding to the C-O vibrations and C=O stretching of TA (Figure S4). The result is attributed to the large molecular size (≈1700 Da) of TA, which is hard to diffuse inside MOFs and functionalized on the surface of MOFs.[21] The synchrotron TeraHz IR spectra, further confirmed the Zn-N vibrational mode (~280 cm⁻¹), aromatic ring deformation (~680 cm⁻¹) and ligand binding mode (~420 cm⁻¹) from ZIF-L, which did not experience detectable changes after TA etching, suggesting the molecular structure of ZIF-L was well preserved (Figure 1d). The 1D 13C NMR spectrum
demonstrated the sustained intensity of the enzyme signal before and after the TA etching, suggesting negligible enzyme leaching during the etching process (Figure 1e and Table S1). Furthermore, Figure 1e (ii) shows the additional \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR peaks of the TA, confirming the successful TA coating. In addition, the NMR lineshapes of the ZIF-L (which are dependent on the framework crystallinity) are identical for the pristine and etched frameworks, demonstrating the TA etching process does not disrupt the overall structure of the MOF. 2D \(^{13}\mathrm{C}-^{1}\mathrm{H}\) heteronuclear correlation (HETCOR) NMR shows signal transfer from the Hα and the NH protons to the ZIF-L, corresponding to the signal transfer from the enzyme to the ZIF-L \((\mathsf{E}\!\to\!\mathsf{Z})\) , while the opposite signal, i.e. from \(Z{\rightarrow}E\) , that is not as intense was also observed due to the \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) signal of the enzyme is \(\mathord{\sim}10\) -fold weaker than that of the ZIF-L (Figure S5, Table S2). Intermolecular correlation signals from the ZIF-L and enzyme to the TA were also observed (Figure 1f), demonstrating the nanometer scale proximity among all three components. The above results indicated that hierarchically porous biocatalytic MOF can be generated by the TA etching process, which was prone to occurring internally without affecting the crystal structure of the MOF.
