The ability to precisely tune the size of newly generated voids is crucial to achieve optimized enzyme conformation and cofactor/substrate shuttling in the microreactors without compromising their stability. Here, the effect of different TA etching time was evaluated using GOx-HRP bi-enzyme-ZIF-L as a model system (Figure S6). During the etching process, the pH of reaction solution is 6.59 (Figure S7), which will not affect the enzyme activity (the pure GOx solution is pH 6.63 and HRP is 7.07). TEM analysis revealed that, with increasing TA etching time (from 0.5 to 2 min), small and discrete voids were transitioned to larger and more interconnected voids (Figure 2a (i-iii)). Eventually the prolonged TA etching resulted in a hollow thin shell structured ZIF-L (Figure 2a (iv)). PXRD and synchrotron SAXS patterns confirmed the pristine ZIF-L crystal structure was well preserved after TA treatment for different durations (Figure S8 and S9). Synchrotron SAXS analysis further revealed the increased scattering intensity of the equatorial center (Figure 2b), suggesting the enzyme-ZIF-L after TA etching for 0.5, 1, and 2 min possessed meso porosity and the pore size increased with the extension of TA etching time. Further analysis of the scattering patterns showed that the amount of small micropores decreased while the large mesopores arose with the extension of TA etching time for 0.5, 1, and 2 min, with a mean diameter of around 6.7, 7.5 and \(8.3\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Figure 2c, Figure S10). Furthermore, the \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) adsorption was steady at low relative pressure, indicating the main microporosity of ZIF-L was not destroyed by etching, while a distinct hysteresis loop of TA-treated bi-enzyme-ZIF-L presented at high relative pressure (Figure S11), which further confirmed the generation of mesopores. The total pore volume of bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA also increased with prolonged etching time (Table S3), further confirming the successful etching of TA.
To investigate whether the change of pore size could affect the catalytic performance, glucose and \(o\)-penylenediamine dihydrochloride (OPD) were introduced in the bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA suspension (Figure 2d), and the formed yellowish 2,3-diaminophenazine (DAP) product was monitored at \(420\ \mathrm{nm}\) by UV-Vis spectroscopy. The successful retention of GOx and HRP inside the hierarchically porous microreactors was confirmed by confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) (Figure S12a). Fluorescent spectrophotometry results showed \(87\%\) GOx and \(93\%\) HRP were immobilized in ZIF-L; while after the TA treatment, the encapsulation efficiency of GOx and HRP was slightly reduced to \(86\%\) and \(90\%\) (Figure S12b), suggesting negligible enzymes leaching during the TA etching process. While all the etched samples demonstrated improved biocatalytic efficiency than the pristine biocatalytic MOF, the optimal bi-enzyme cascade activity was achieved under the condition of a TA etching time of 1 min and a TA concentration of \(15\ \mathrm{mg/mL}\), which was 2.2-time higher than the pristine biocatalytic MOF (Figure 2e, Figure S13). Control experiments of ZIF-L and ZIF-L-TA displayed no enzyme activity (Figure S14). Further decreasing the TA concentration from \(15\ \mathrm{mg/L}\) to \(10\ \mathrm{mg/mL}\) or increasing to \(18\ \mathrm{mg/mL}\) led to the decreased enzymatic activity (Figure S15). When bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA was
subject to a shorter etching time or lower TA concentration, substrates and intermediates diffusion could still be limited due to incomplete etching. However, for prolonged TA etching time or higher TA concentration, the external ZIF-L micropores may experience blockage by excessive TA coating on the ZIF-L surface (Figure S16 and 17) as demonstrated previously (average TA molecular weight \(\approx\!1700\,\mathsf{D a}\)).[21]
The interfacial interactions between the enzymes and the hierarchically porous MOF structure was studied. FTIR spectrum confirmed that the enzymes were encapsulated in ZIF-L-TA, displaying two peaks in the \(1560~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) and \(1700~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) spectral regions where (i) the amide I peak \((1700{-}1610\;\mathsf{c m}^{-1})\) overlapped with the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) \((1700\;\mathsf{c m}^{-1})\) of TA, and (ii) the ZIF-L peak centered at \(1560~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) overlapped with the amide II band (1595-1480 \(\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\)) of the enzymes (Figure S16). To improve the separation of overlapping peaks, the second derivative spectra were further explored.[42] Usually, the amide I band in the FTIR spectrum is utilized to evaluate the secondary structure of enzymes, because it possess the most intense signal.[43] Compared to the
pure enzymes, a notable shift in the enzymes spectral bands was observed in the bi-enzyme-ZIF-L and bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA systems (Figure 2f). For enzymes in ZIF-L, the amide I peak which is mainly corresponding to the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) stretching vibration showed a blue shift from \(1650~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) (soluble free enzymes) to \(1680~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) owing to its specific or non-specific interaction with ZIF-L. However, after TA etching, the amide I peak of the enzymes presented a red shift to \(1630~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\), which is associated with the enzymes structure reorientation to spread in the large mesopores in their lower energy form. Another complementary characterization technique, solid state NMR, was further employed to reveal the atomic-level interaction between the enzymes and the MOF during the etching process. Comparing the signals of the Ha (NCH) and the amide NH protons by taking a slice along the vertical F1 \((^1\mathsf{H})\) dimension at the position of the imidazole \(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\) position in Figure S5b and d, the signal is much higher in the etched MOF system than in the unetched system (Figure 2g). This implies that the components of the enzyme are on average much closer to the ZIF-L in the etched system than in the unetched system. This observation provides an insight into the formation and structure of the enzymes-MOF systems. When the ZIF-L framework is formed in a solution containing the enzymes, the enzymes is in its confined or non-native hydrated state with a resultant smaller interfacial area.[20] Conversely, with the generation of larger mesopores by etching, the enzymes is able to reorient in a manner that lowers their surface energy. This is primarily achieved by the enzymes spreading along the surface of the larger cavity via strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding with the imidazole moieties resulting in an increased interfacial contact, thereby a much more intense intermolecular correlation peaks appeared. Further evidence is provided by the residual intramolecular correlation signal of the enzymes in the unetched system (Figure S5b), which indicates that on the 10 ms time scale, the \(^1\mathsf{H}\) signal of the enzymes have not had sufficient time to diffuse completely into the ZIF-L due to smaller interfacial contact. In contrast, a high interfacial contact presented in the etched system, where \(^1\mathsf{H}\) signal of the enzymes is efficiently transferred into the MOF (highlighted by the dashed circle) (Figure S5d). Consequently, consistent results from FTIR and solid-state NMR reveal the hierarchically porous MOF enables the enzymes to reorientate and spread in their lower surface energy form, forming stronger coordination with the MOF framework.
To demonstrate the versatility of this controlled etching method towards enhancing more complex biocatalysis, the capacity for conducting a three-enzyme cascade reaction and cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalysis within the hierarchically
porous ZIF-L microreactors was further explored. For the three-enzyme system, the biocatalytic cascade was initiated by lactose hydrolysis catalyzed by \(\beta\)-galactosidase (\(\beta\)-Gal), generating glucose for the subsequent reactions catalyzed by GOx and HRP (Figure 3a). CLSM confirmed the different fluorophores-labeled \(\beta\)-Gal, GOx, and HRP were co-localized within the three-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA microreactors and experienced no spatial distributions change after TA etching (Figure S18a and b). Over \(70\%\) of the three enzymes were co-immobilized into the ZIF-L and negligible enzyme leaching was observed after TA etching (Figure S18c). The crystal structure of three-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA was preserved (Figure S19). However, the hindered reaction rates were significant for these microreactors, presenting an initial lag phase at the start of the biocatalytic reaction, which may be attributed to the relatively large molecular size of the lactose substrate (estimated dimension \(11 \times 7.8 \times 4 \, \text{Å}^3\)), where its diffusion is prone to suppression by the narrow ZIF-L pores (estimated average pore size of \(9.4 \times 7 \times 5.3 \, \text{Å}^3\))[47,48] and intercrystal spaces. After TA etching, all the hierarchically porous ZIF-L-TA microreactors showed significant improvement in cascade kinetics than the dense, pristine biocatalytic ZIF-L. As shown in Figure 3b and Figure S20, the highest reaction rate and conversion efficiency was achieved with the 1 min TA treated sample, showing an impressively 16-fold higher relative biocascade activity than the microreactors before etching, whereas further increasing or decreasing the etching time resulted in a less optimized relative activity. Further decreasing or increasing the TA concentration from \(15 \, \text{mg/mL}\) to \(10 \, \text{mg/mL}\) or \(18 \, \text{mg/mL}\) also resulted in a decreased activity (Figure S21). Compared to the bi-enzyme cascade reaction, the greater enhancement of the three-enzyme cascade reaction suggests that the optimized hierarchical porosity within ZIF-L-TA microreactors is more critical for complex multi-enzyme systems. This is largely attributed to the necessity for efficient substrates and intermediates shuttling as well as the optimized multienzyme reorientation within ZIF-L. Nevertheless, excessive etching can compromise the enzymatic activity of the microreactor system. The diminished performance of biocatalytic MOF at 2 minutes etching was attributed to the resulted poor mechanical properties of the over-etched samples, leading to a reduction in the integrity of microreactors (Figure S22). The result suggests that the three-enzyme system is more fragile and unstable than the two-enzyme system during prolonged TA etching, which could be associated to the different capabilities of enzymes toward inducing the MOF mineralization process.[5] Thus, a compromise between enlargement of the internal voids and acceptable mechanical properties of the microreactors is critical to deliver the highly reactive heterogenous biocatalysts.
Cofactor-dependent biocatalysis is then assessed by employing the \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\) co-enzyme and L-glutamate dehydrogenase (L-GluDH) co-immobilized within ZIF-L, which is denoted as \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\)-L-GluDH-ZIF-L, for catalyzing the conversion of L-glutamic acid to \(\alpha\)-ketoglutarate (Figure 3c). CLSM images confirmed that the L-GluDH and \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\) cofactors were successfully retained in the ZIF-L microreactor after TA treatment (Figure S23a) and approximately \(72.6\% \, \text{NAD^{+}}\) and \(71.2\% \, \text{L-GluDH}\) were encapsulated within the ZIF-L-TA microreactor, which was comparable to \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\)-L-GluDH-ZIF-L before etching (Figure S23b). Furthermore, there was no enzyme-cofactor cascade reaction observed in the supernatant
after TA etching with extra addition of L-GluDH (Figure S24), confirming negligible cofactor was released from the hierarchically porous ZIF-L. The MOF crystal structure was well preserved (Figure S25). The \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\)-dependent biocatalytic reaction was quantitatively assessed by UV-vis spectrophotometry at \(664 \, \text{nm}\) using methylene blue (MB+) reagent, which penetrated through the microreactors and was immediately reduced to propagated methylene blue (MBH) by the converted NADH from \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\).[50] The cofactor-dependent enzymatic reaction was swift in the hierarchically porous ZIF-L-TA microreactors (Figure S26), while the \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\)-L-GluDH-ZIF-L without TA-etching offered a slower reaction kinetic, possibly due to the limited diffusion of substrates and the confined enzymes and cofactors in ZIF-L. Compared to the pristine microreactors, the TA treated counterparts exhibited a 1.7-fold higher enzyme activity when the TA etching time was 1 min, whereas a further increase or decrease of the etching time resulted in a less optimized \(\mathsf{N A D^{+}}\) conversion rate (Figure 3d). To further verify the versatility of the hierarchically porous microreactor, another cofactor-dependent enzymatic reaction was investigated by employing formate dehydrogenase (FDH) and NADH for catalyzing the \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) conversion to formic acid,[50] which is a key step in the biocatalytic \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) conversion to useful chemicals or fuels (i.e. methanol). NADH cofactor was employed as the critical electron donor via FDH (Figure 3e). CLSM images confirmed the NADH and FDH were successfully immobilized in ZIF-L and were well-retained after treatment by TA (Figure S27a, b). Around \(83\% \, \text{NADH}\) and \(76\% \, \text{FDH}\) were encapsulated in the NADH-FDH-ZIF-L-TA system (Figure S27b). There was no enzyme-cofactor cascade reaction observed in the supernatant after TA etching with extra addition of FDH (Figure S28), confirming negligible cofactor was released during the TA etching process. PXRD spectra revealed that the crystal structure of the microreactor was well maintained after TA etching (Figure S29). After the optimal TA etching time of 1 min, up to 6.6-fold higher biocatalytic activity was achieved relative to the pristine NADH-FDH-ZIF-L (Figure 3f). The multienzyme activity enhancements are comparable to previous reports (Table S4, note that cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalysis enhancement has not been reported in the literature). Therefore, the hierarchically porous biocatalytic MOF microreactor could be employed as a versatile platform towards improved multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalysis.
The capacity of these microreactors operating in adverse environments was finally evaluated. After incubating the catalysts at pH 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7, the activity of bi-enzyme-ZIF-L decreased with decreased pH and dramatically drop to \(29\%\) after acidic treatment at pH 3 (Figure S30). Notably, bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA offered excellent protection to acidic pH compared to ZIF-L. Even after the treatment of pH 3 for 0.5 h, \(68.7\%\) of the original activity was still maintained (Figure 4a). Furthermore, the effect of different temperature on the bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA microreactors was also examined. Figure S30 shows that the activity of bi-enzyme-ZIF-L decreased with increased temperature, and \(53\%\) of its original activity was retained at 90 \(^\circ\mathrm{C}\), whereas bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA maintained \(84\%\) of its original activity at the same condition (Figure 4a). In addition, over \(90\%\) of the enzymatic cascade activity of bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA was retained after digestive enzyme \((2.5\ \mathrm{mg/mL}\) trypsin) treatment for \(3\ \mathrm{h}\), whereas only \(62\%\) of the activity for bi-enzyme-ZIF-L was recorded, and the free bi-enzyme system became inactive quickly (Figure 4b and Figure S31). Finally, after using the bi-enzyme-ZIF-L-TA system for five consecutive cycles, over \(80\%\)
of its initial activity was successfully retained (Figure S32), and the crystal structure of the MOF was not affected (Figure S33). These results suggest that the ZIF-L-TA offers superior protection to enzymes than pristine ZIF-L, which was collectively derived from the "shielding" effect from the MOF framework,[5] the symbiotic stability reinforcement effect between enzymes and ZIF-L matrix,[20] as well as the marked stability enhancement offered by the TA coating.[21] Importantly, the stronger coordinative interaction between the enzymes and the MOF framework induced by TA coating as evidenced from the solid-state NMR (Figure 2g and S5) resulted a more rigid enzyme structure, which was less susceptible to conformational changes under harsh environments. The capacity of these hierarchically porous biocatalytic MOF microreactors against inhospitable environments offers the opportunity of utilizing native and complex biocascade reactions for the large-scale and commercial use with high catalytic performance and stability.
## Conclusion
In conclusion, hierarchically porous biocatalytic MOF microreactors were developed via a simple TA-etching strategy to improve the catalytic efficiency of immobilized multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalytic reactions. The enhanced multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalysis was resulted from the reorientation of enzymes in the etched mesopores, which lowers the enzymes surface energy yet still presents strong intermolecular interaction between the enzymes and the MOF framework, and the decreased barriers to accelerate the shuttling of small reactants and intermediates. The results demonstrated that the TA etching conditions played key roles in controlling the pore size of the biocatalytic ZIF-L and contributed significantly to improving the enzymatic cascade activity. Under the optimal etching condition, the efficiency of multienzyme cascade and cofactor-dependent enzyme biocatalytic reactions were significantly improved up to 16-fold. The hierarchically porous MOF microreactors also presented superior stability against variations in pH, temperature, and in the presence of proteolysis, and demonstrated superior recyclability for sustained uses. Collectively, the results presented in this work introduces a versatile strategy for fabricating optimized hierarchically porous MOF microreactors as heterogeneous biocatalytic platforms toward efficient multienzyme and cofactor-dependent enzyme cascade reactions
and offers new capability for diverse biotechnological applications.
